Unit 11 Individual Project
Project Title

Comparative Intelligence and Ethics in Machine Learning: Classical Matrix Factorisation versus Neural Collaborative Filtering

Project Purpose

My individual project compared two recommendation approaches using the MovieLens 100K dataset:

regularised biased matrix factorisation;
neural collaborative filtering.

The project did not simply ask which model achieved the lowest error. It examined whether the additional complexity of the neural model produced a stable and meaningful benefit.

The evaluation considered:

rating prediction;
ranking quality;
validation stability;
explainability;
subgroup performance;
privacy;
computational cost;
deployment risk.
Dataset

MovieLens 100K contains:

100,000 ratings;
943 users;
1,682 films;
ratings from 1 to 5.

The dataset was suitable because it is a recognised benchmark and can be processed on an ordinary computer.

However, it also has limitations. The ratings were collected in the 1990s and therefore do not represent current streaming catalogues, modern interfaces or contemporary user behaviour.

Missing ratings are also ambiguous. A user may not have watched a film, may not know it exists or may have intentionally avoided it.

Data Preparation

I loaded the rating, user and item data and mapped user and film identifiers to continuous numerical indices.

The same data split was used for both models.

For each user:

earlier ratings were used for training;
the second most recent rating was used for validation;
the latest rating was used for testing.

This temporal split reduced the risk of future-to-past leakage and better represented a real recommendation scenario.

I also checked:

missing core values;
duplicate user-item pairs;
valid rating ranges;
consistent preprocessing maps.
Classical Model: Matrix Factorisation

The classical model was regularised biased matrix factorisation.

Its prediction combined:

the global average rating;
a user bias;
a film bias;
the interaction between user and film latent vectors.

The model used:

20 latent factors;
stochastic gradient descent;
L2 regularisation;
early stopping;
rating clipping between 1 and 5.

The model was computationally efficient and its prediction could be decomposed into interpretable components.

Deep Model: Neural Collaborative Filtering

The neural model used separate user and film embeddings.

The embeddings were concatenated and passed through dense neural-network layers with:

64 ReLU units;
32 ReLU units;
dropout;
weight decay;
Adam optimisation;
learning-rate reduction;
early stopping.

The output was transformed back to the 1–5 rating scale.

The model had greater non-linear capacity but also introduced more parameters, longer training time and greater explanation difficulty.

Evaluation

I used two main performance metrics.

RMSE

RMSE measured how close the predicted ratings were to the observed ratings.

Lower RMSE represented better rating prediction.

NDCG@10

NDCG@10 measured whether relevant films appeared near the top of a recommendation list.

A held-out film with a rating of at least four was treated as relevant and compared with sampled unseen items.

This was important because rating prediction and ranking quality are different objectives.

Validation Strategy

I used the temporal validation and test structure as the main evaluation design.

I also included a three-fold sensitivity analysis within the development data.

The purpose was to determine whether performance was stable or depended on a convenient data split.

This improved on the single-split limitation identified in the Airbnb group project.

Explainability

For matrix factorisation, I produced a local additive explanation using:

global mean;
user bias;
film bias;
latent interaction.

These components reconstructed the predicted rating.

For neural collaborative filtering, I used permutation importance by shuffling user or film identifiers and measuring the increase in RMSE.

This showed whether the model relied more heavily on user identity or item identity.

However, I recognised that permutation importance indicates reliance rather than causality.

Bias Analysis

I examined whether model error differed across:

low-activity users;
medium-activity users;
high-activity users;
age categories;
the dataset’s historical gender categories.

The demographic variables were used only after prediction and were not used as model inputs.

I also considered group sample sizes and the limitations of the dataset’s historical demographic categories.

I learned that equal RMSE does not necessarily mean equal recommendation quality, catalogue exposure or user experience.

Ethical Considerations

The project examined several ethical risks:

privacy;
re-identification;
popularity bias;
false-positive recommendations;
false-negative recommendations;
feedback loops;
unequal exposure;
limited user control.

Although MovieLens uses pseudonymous identifiers, combinations of demographic and behavioural data could still increase re-identification risk.

A production system should therefore include:

data minimisation;
encryption;
access controls;
retention limits;
deletion options;
user correction;
recommendation dismissal;
opt-out mechanisms.
Deployment Design

The proposed deployment included:

an authenticated client request;
candidate-film retrieval;
model scoring;
a policy layer;
ranked results;
monitoring;
human approval;
fallback models;
rollback capability.

The system would monitor:

latency;
failures;
data drift;
score drift;
coverage;
subgroup NDCG;
exposure concentration;
user feedback.
Final Recommendation

My recommendation was to begin with matrix factorisation.

For a dataset of this size, it provides:

strong baseline performance;
lower computational cost;
smaller model size;
direct prediction decomposition;
predictable deployment.

Neural collaborative filtering should only be selected if it demonstrates a meaningful and stable ranking improvement that justifies its additional cost, latency and explanation burden.

The main conclusion was that complexity must earn its place through reproducible evidence.
